on |","+| `pathwidth_le_of_bisectionTreeMax_two_mul` (**C2**, the item's form) | `… → pw G ≤ k + 2 * bs.sum` | the factor 2 is slack |","+","+Three choices beyond §2.6, each the smaller thing. (i) **C1s is proved in","+the relative form** (`S₁ ∪ S₂` with `boundaryTo`, no complement), and the","+global form is its corollary at `(S, Sᶜ)`: the iterated form then recurses","+on `Finset V` with the one graph `G`, and the nested `G[S][S₁]` that §2.6","+anticipated (\"`induce_induce` or `pathwidth_eq_of_iso`\") never appears. The","+only nested `induce` in the file is inside the deletion lemma, where","+`pathwidth_le_of_injective` takes a hand-written injective homomorphism.","+(ii) **The deletion lemma needs no inclusion** (§4.1). (iii)","+**`BisectionTreeMax` gives `k + Σ b`, not `k + 2 Σ b`**: budgeting the larger","+boundary budgets the smaller, so the item's factor 2 is pure slack; both","+forms are in the file so that the item's text has its lemma. Mathlib","+supplied `induceHomOfLE` and `induceUnivIso`; the project's `TreeBound.lean`","+supplied the two lemmas C1s rests on, as §2.1 (2) said.","+","+### 4.3 What Fomin–Høie's one-level argument adds (stated, not proved)","+","+Iterating C2s with Monien–Preis cuts of `(1/6 + ε)` of each piece sums to","+`n/3`, not `n/6` (plan §0.4 (3)). Fomin & Høie (2006, Theorem 5) reach","+`(1/6 + ε) n` for maximum degree 3 with **one** level and two ingredients","+that are not in `CutLemma.lean`, quoted from the plan's §0.2 (their IPL","+paper is held):","+","+> **Lemma 4** (Fomin & Høie 2006). Let `G` be a graph on `n` vertices and with","+> maximum vertex degree at most 3. Then for any vertex subset `X ⊆ V(G)` there","+> is a path decomposition `P = (X₁, X₂, …, X_r)` of `G` of width at most","+> `max{|X|, n/3 + 1} + (2/3) log₃ n + 1` and such that `X = X_r`.","+","+With a bisection `V₁, V₂` of at most `(1/6 + ε/2) n` cut edges (Monien &","+Preis, their Theorem 2) they take Lemma 4 on `G[V₁]` with `X = ∂V₁` as the","+*last* bag and on `G[V₂]` with `X = ∂V₂` as the *first* bag — each of width","+at most `max{(1/6 + ε/2) n, n/6 + 1} + (2/3) log₃ n + 1 ≤ (1/6 + ε) n` — and","+join them through a **sweep** `P₂ = (B₁, …, B_r)` of `G[∂V₁ ∪ ∂V₂]` with","+`B₁ = ∂V₁`, `B_r = ∂V₂`: at an odd `j` pick `v ∈ B_j ∖ ∂V₂`, put","+`B_{j+1} = B_j ∪ (N(v) ∩ ∂V₂)` and `B_{j+2} = B_{j+1} ∖ {v}`; counting the","+vertices of `∂V₁` by their number `m` of neighbours in `∂V₂`","+(`|D₁| + 2|D₂| + 3|D₃| ≤ (1/6 + ε) n`), every bag stays within","+`|D₂| + 2|D₃| + 1` of `|B₁|`, so `|B_j| ≤ (1/6 + ε) n + 1`. In the vocabulary","+of this file, the sweep is a path decomposition of `G[∂V₁ ∪ ∂V₂]` whose bags","+are at most `|∂V₁| + 1`, i.e. the middle bag `∂V₁ ∪ ∂V₂` of C1's third term","+is replaced by a sequence of bags each of size about `|∂V₁|`, and Lemma 4","+makes the boundary the end bag of each side so that the three pieces","+concatenate. As a lemma in the file's terms:","+","+> **Lemma (Fomin–Høie transfer; stated, not proved).** For `G` of maximum","+> degree 3 and a partition `V = V₁ ⊔ V₂` with `c` cut edges,","+> `pw(G) ≤ max{c, n/6 + 1} + (2/3) log₃ n + 2`.","+","+Its proof needs Lemma 4 (a decomposition of a degree-3 graph with a","+prescribed end bag, whose `n/3 + 1` and `(2/3) log₃ n` come from Theorem 3,","+`pw(T) ≤ log₃ n` for trees — `tree_pathwidth_le_log` in `TreeBound.lean` is","+that tree case) and the sweep. Neither is attempted here; both are a","+candidate for item 11. C1 and C2 are what a general graph gives with no","+degree bound; they are not how Fomin–Høie reach `n/6`.","+","+### 4.4 Kill verdict, and what this does not claim","+","+**Kill verdict: not met.** No counterexample in the check (every graph to 8","+vertices for the one-cut statements, 10,000 random trees for the iterated","+ones); C2 fitted in Lean in the list-indexed form, so the fallback \"C1 alone,","+C2 stated in the report\" was not needed.","+","+**Does not claim.** Fomin–Høie's `n/6` (§4.3: Lemma 4 and the sweep, not","+C1–C2)